  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains anchored over the region over the next few\ndays, yielding an ongoing stretch of dry weather and warming\ndaytime temperatures. Thermal profiles illustrates a vast layer\nof dry air present this week while the surface high retains\nresidency overhead. This will translate to low afternoon RH \nvalues, especially along the higher elevations. Fire weather\nconcerns remain minimal this week amid lack of surface and low\nlevel wind flow. Additionally, early morning river valley fog\nchances lessen each day this week in response to the plethora of\ndry air in place.\n\nForecast trends remain on track for the prolonged stretch of dry\nweather to come to a conclusion by the end of the weekend. The\ndominating ridge breaks down late in the work week to give rise\nto upper level troughing over the Intermountain West that will\nsend a cold front eastward into the eastern half of the country.\nAs it currently stands, a light wetting rain will be the main\nhighlight from this disturbance, especially with very dry\nantecedent conditions this system will initially have to\novercome.",
